Skill "code-review" completed (forked execution).

Result:
Eight findings, ranked most-severe first. I ran the checks below against the PR head (`c12d480`) with ESBMC and did not apply any fixes. `--comment` was not acted on: no inline-comment tool was available and the findings aren't posted to PR #405.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/grading/equivalence.py",
    "line": 778,
    "summary": "extract_signature runs on the raw source (comments included) while every other scan uses the comment-masked view, so a commented-out signature before the real definition becomes the harness signature.",
    "failure_scenario": "Both sources start with `/* was: char f(long a) { */ int f(int a){...}` and are otherwise identical. extract_signature matches the comment, so the harness draws `long a` and stores the result in `char __forseti_ret = f(a)`. The harness I rendered did exactly this. Two mutants that differ only in the upper return bits then compare as equal, so a killable mutant is reported EquivalenceVerified and skipped, which inflates the kill-rate. The shape check does not catch it because the same stale comment is in both sources. Fix: call extract_signature on mask_comments(source), or on the view."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/grading/equivalence.py",
    "line": 330,
    "summary": "The reference and candidate are pasted into one translation unit verbatim, so include guards, `#pragma`, and any preprocessor state in the reference silently change the candidate. A function the candidate calls but never defines is turned into a nondet value, so identical sources get a spurious VIOLATED verdict.",
    "failure_scenario": "Reference and candidate are both `#ifndef G / #define G / int f(int a){return a+1;} / #endif`. The candidate body is skipped by the guard. `f` is then undefined and ESBMC returns nondet, so check_equivalence(ref, ref) returns EquivalenceViolated with a witness. This happened when I ran it. A pair that both call an undefined extern `int ext(int)` also returns VIOLATED, because each call draws an independent nondet. A mutant with a typo'd or missing helper is likewise 'killed' without ever compiling. Fix: refuse sources with include-guard `#ifndef`/`#if` blocks, or run ESBMC with `--error-on-undefined-function` and map the result to UNKNOWN or refused."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/grading/equivalence.py",
    "line": 541,
    "summary": "filter_equivalent only checks that the original can be rendered. It never checks that the reference is fault-free, so a faulting original makes every mutant Refused after a full ESBMC run each.",
    "failure_scenario": "The original is `int f(int a,int b){return a/b;}`. _prepare(original, original) passes, but each mutant check ends in the 'reference itself violates a property' refusal (division by zero in f__ref). The filter then runs one ESBMC process (up to the 30s timeout) per mutant and skips nothing. I reproduced the refusal. A single check_equivalence(original, original) up front would detect this and set original_refusal once. Better still, the harness could constrain the reference's faulting inputs so the pair can still be compared."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/grading/equivalence.py",
    "line": 296,
    "summary": "Buffers declared `const` are never compared, but a unit can still write through a cast, so that divergence is invisible and the pair is reported VERIFIED.",
    "failure_scenario": "Reference is `int f(const int *a,int n){int *p=(int*)a; if(n>0)p[0]=7; return 0;}` and the candidate is `return 0;`. They differ in observable memory, but only the return value is asserted. I got EquivalenceVerified. The mutant is skipped and the kill-rate is inflated. Comparing const buffers too is cheap, since the seed copy already exists."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/grading/equivalence.py",
    "line": 666,
    "summary": "`_nondet` re-implements the nondet-name slug that already exists.",
    "failure_scenario": "`properties/harness.py::_nondet_slug` and `precond/synth.py::_nondet_slug` already compute `\"nondet_\" + re.sub(r\"[^A-Za-z0-9_]\", \"_\", ctype.strip())`. A third copy means a change to the naming convention (for example, which names ESBMC treats as nondet) has to be made in three places. Export and reuse the existing helper."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/grading/equivalence.py",
    "line": 415,
    "summary": "Unknown reasons are converted with `UnknownReason[result.reason.name]`, which couples to the ESBMC enum's member names and raises KeyError if the ESBMC enum gains a member.",
    "failure_scenario": "The ESBMC-side UnknownReason gets a new reason, such as a solver-error reason. _classify then raises KeyError, so check_equivalence and every filter_equivalent call crash instead of returning EquivalenceUnknown. Fall back to UNCLASSIFIED for names this enum lacks."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/grading/equivalence.py",
    "line": 547,
    "summary": "Mutant checks run strictly sequentially, and each one re-runs _prepare on the same original, which is a full scan and rename.",
    "failure_scenario": "With 200 mutants and a 30s timeout each, the worst case is about 100 minutes of wall time. The runs are independent and could use a bounded thread pool. The original's scan, signature and renamed text are recomputed per mutant even though they never change, so they could be cached once per filter_equivalent call."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/grading/equivalence.py",
    "line": 519,
    "summary": "to_dict()['verdicts'] is keyed by mutant id, so duplicate ids silently overwrite each other, while `kept` and `skipped_equivalent` keep every entry.",
    "failure_scenario": "Two mutants share an id, for example the same operator applied at two sites with a non-unique id. 'verdicts' keeps only the last one, so the JSON report disagrees with its own kept and skipped counts. The result for a skipped mutant can be replaced by the verdict of a kept one."
  }
]
```

